        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p><strong>Wireshark</strong> is the industry standard for packet capture and network protocol analysis. It captures frames from a network interface and decodes the encapsulated packets into human-readable formats.</p>
            
            <h3>Capture Filters vs. Display Filters</h3>
            <p>It is critical not to confuse these two distinct filter engines, as they use completely different syntax:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Capture Filters:</strong> Configured <em>before</em> starting a capture. They use Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) syntax (e.g., <code>host 192.0.2.10</code>). They drop unwanted traffic at the hardware/OS level, saving disk space and memory.</li>
                <li><strong>Display Filters:</strong> Configured <em>during or after</em> a capture. They use Wireshark's proprietary display filter syntax. They hide traffic from the screen without deleting it from the capture file.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Common Display Filters</h3>
            <p>When analyzing a large capture, display filters are essential:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><code>ip.addr == 192.0.2.10</code> : Shows traffic sourced from or destined to that IP.</li>
                <li><code>tcp.port == 443</code> : Shows traffic involving port 443.</li>
                <li><code>dns</code> : Shows only DNS query/response traffic.</li>
                <li><code>icmp</code> : Shows ICMP traffic (pings, error messages).</li>
                <li><code>tcp.flags.syn == 1</code> : Shows TCP packets attempting to initiate a handshake.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Basic Troubleshooting</h3>
            <p>By observing raw packets, you can definitively prove network behavior. For example, you can observe a <a href="dns-record-types-explained.html">DNS</a> query leaving an interface and verify if the response actually arrives. You can observe the three-way TCP handshake to confirm a remote server is listening, or look for TCP Retransmissions indicating network drops.</p>
            
            <h3>Security and Privacy Considerations</h3>
            <p>Packet captures inherently contain sensitive data, including IP addresses, hostnames, application payloads, cookies, and credentials if insecure protocols are used. Always advise safe handling, obtain authorization before capturing traffic, and never use packet capture for unauthorized credential sniffing.</p>
        
        </div>
        
